add_blog_entity_and_seed_pipeline.s:
9 full blog posts already exist as real files at `content/blog/*.md` (`omegle-alternatives.md`, `whatsapp-alternatives.md`, `make-new-friends-online.md`, `no-login-chat-apps.md`, `random-chat-apps-compared.md`, `find-chat-groups.md`, `random-video-chat-safety.md`, `x2-vs-omegle-vs-discord.md`, `group-chat-guide.md`) — each with YAML frontmatter (`slug`, `title`, `meta_description`, `keyword`, `secondary_keywords`, `tags`, `published`, `faq: [{q,a}]`) and a full verbatim markdown body. This step's job is purely the ingestion pipeline and the Qdrant entity — the content itself is already written and should not be regenerated or altered by whoever executes this step.

**1. `BlogPost` entity**, structured identically to `Group`/`Sticker` (same `view()`/embed-on-write/search-with-ZV-fallback shape already established twice in the other plan — reuse the pattern a third time rather than inventing a new one). Confirm the `s` discriminator `'bp'` is free (grep every `s: '...'` literal in the repo first — as of the other plan's `add_message_stickers` step, the full set in use was `ce, fo, g, m, mu, sk, sm, u, x` — `bp` should still be free, but re-grep before finalizing since other plan steps may have landed more entities since).

Create `src/lib/server/blog.ts` (new file):
```ts
import { ZV, V, ensure, upsert, retrieve_one, search, scroll, f, eq, type QEnv } from './qdrant';
import { embed } from './or';

export interface BlogPost {
	s: 'bp';
	id: string; // = slug, human-readable ids are fine here since posts are never renamed casually
	slug: string;
	ti: string; // title
	de: string; // meta description
	kw: string; // primary target keyword
	tgs: string[]; // tags (secondary keywords + topical tags combined) — folds into the embedding
	bd: string; // full markdown body
	faq: { q: string; a: string }[];
	d: number; // published ts
}

export type BlogPostView = {
	slug: string;
	title: string;
	description: string;
	keyword: string;
	tags: string[];
	body: string;
	faq: { q: string; a: string }[];
	published: number;
	score?: number;
};

const view = (p: BlogPost, score?: number): BlogPostView => ({
	slug: p.slug,
	title: p.ti,
	description: p.de,
	keyword: p.kw,
	tags: p.tgs ?? [],
	body: p.bd,
	faq: p.faq ?? [],
	published: p.d,
	...(score === undefined ? {} : { score })
});

const post_text = (title: string, keyword: string, tags: string[]) =>
	`post_title: ${title} | post_keyword: ${keyword}${tags.length ? ` | post_tags: ${tags.join(', ')}` : ''}`;

export async function upsert_post(
	env: QEnv,
	data: { slug: string; title: string; description: string; keyword: string; tags: string[]; body: string; faq: { q: string; a: string }[]; published: number }
): Promise<BlogPostView> {
	await ensure(env);
	const p: BlogPost = {
		s: 'bp',
		id: data.slug,
		slug: data.slug,
		ti: data.title,
		de: data.description,
		kw: data.keyword,
		tgs: data.tags,
		bd: data.body,
		faq: data.faq,
		d: data.published
	};
	const vec = await embed(env, post_text(p.ti, p.kw, p.tgs));
	await upsert(env, [{ id: p.id, vector: { [V]: vec }, payload: p as unknown as Record<string, unknown> }]);
	return view(p);
}

export async function get_post(env: QEnv, slug: string): Promise<BlogPostView | null> {
	const p = (await retrieve_one(env, slug))?.payload as unknown as BlogPost | undefined;
	return p?.s === 'bp' ? view(p) : null;
}

export async function list_posts(env: QEnv, limit = 100): Promise<BlogPostView[]> {
	await ensure(env);
	const pts = await scroll(env, f(eq('s', 'bp')), limit);
	return pts.map((p) => view(p.payload as unknown as BlogPost)).sort((a, b) => b.published - a.published);
}

export async function search_posts(env: QEnv, q: string, limit = 10): Promise<BlogPostView[]> {
	await ensure(env);
	if (!q) return list_posts(env, limit);
	const vec = await embed(env, q);
	if (vec === ZV) return list_posts(env, limit);
	const hits = await search(env, vec, f(eq('s', 'bp')), limit);
	return hits.map((h) => view(h.payload as unknown as BlogPost, h.score));
}
```
`id: data.slug` (not `new_id()`/a UUID) is deliberate — a blog post's URL slug IS its natural stable identifier, re-running the seed script for the same file must upsert the same point rather than creating a duplicate, and human-readable ids make debugging/manually inspecting the Qdrant collection far easier for content that's meant to be read by humans anyway. No new `KEYWORD_KEYS` entry needed — filtering is only ever by `s: 'bp'` (already indexed), same reasoning as `Sticker`'s tags.

**2. Frontmatter parsing.** Add dependency: `pnpm add gray-matter` (confirmed real, v4.0.3, the standard, minimal frontmatter parser — do not hand-roll YAML parsing).

**3. Seed script.** Create `scripts/seed-blog.ts` (new file, run via a `tsx`/`node --experimental-strip-types` invocation — check this repo's `scripts/` directory for an existing script to match its exact invocation convention, e.g. how it's already run/what runtime it targets, before finalizing this file's shebang/imports):
```ts
import { readdirSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import matter from 'gray-matter';
import { upsert_post } from '../src/lib/server/blog';
import type { QEnv } from '../src/lib/server/qdrant';

const CONTENT_DIR = join(process.cwd(), 'content/blog');

export async function seed_blog(env: QEnv): Promise<{ slug: string }[]> {
	const files = readdirSync(CONTENT_DIR).filter((f) => f.endsWith('.md'));
	const results: { slug: string }[] = [];
	for (const file of files) {
		const raw = readFileSync(join(CONTENT_DIR, file), 'utf-8');
		const { data, content } = matter(raw);
		await upsert_post(env, {
			slug: data.slug,
			title: data.title,
			description: data.meta_description,
			keyword: data.keyword,
			tags: [...(data.secondary_keywords ?? []), ...(data.tags ?? [])],
			body: content.trim(),
			faq: data.faq ?? [],
			published: new Date(data.published).getTime()
		});
		results.push({ slug: data.slug });
	}
	return results;
}
```
This needs real `QEnv` credentials (`QDRANT_URL`/`QDRANT_KEY`) to run standalone outside a request context — check how this repo's other one-off scripts (if any exist under `scripts/`) currently obtain those (likely reading from `.env` directly via `dotenv` or Node's built-in `--env-file`, since there's no live `event.platform.env` outside a Worker request) before finalizing the script's entry point; wire whatever pattern already exists rather than inventing a new one. Confirm all 9 slugs (`omegle-alternatives`, `whatsapp-alternatives`, `make-new-friends-online`, `no-login-chat-apps`, `random-chat-apps-compared`, `find-chat-groups`, `random-video-chat-safety`, `x2-vs-omegle-vs-discord`, `group-chat-guide`) match each file's actual frontmatter `slug:` value exactly before running.

